Introduction
Every correlation dashboard on this site answers the same question: how strongly are these instruments moving together right now? That number is useful, but it is a level, and a level cannot say whether the market still has any internal structure left. A market where metals move with metals, indices with indices, and the yen moves against everything is different from one where all twenty-six instruments collapse onto a single driver. Yet both can report the same average correlation.
Sandhu, Georgiou and Tannenbaum made the case in 2016 that the missing quantity is a geometric one. Build a network whose nodes are the instruments and whose edge lengths come from their correlations, then ask a question from Riemannian geometry: is this network locally curved? On a positively curved edge the endpoints share most of their neighborhoods, so there are many ways round and no connection is load bearing. On a negatively curved edge there is one way across, and cutting it splits the market in two. Curvature is a statement about
shape
that barely moves with the correlation level, which is the property no correlation average can have.
This article builds that measurement in MQL5 from nothing. The definition used is Ollivier's:
kappa(x, y) =
1
- W1(m_x, m_y) / d(x, y)
where d is the distance between two instruments, m_x is a probability measure spread over x's neighborhood, and W1 is the optimal transport cost of turning one of those measures into the other. Everything in the library is arithmetic around that one quantity, so the transport solver is the foundation and is solved exactly.
One warning belongs at the top, because it shapes every design choice below. The raw curvature value has no standalone interpretation: it sits on a compressed scale whose structureless reference depends only on the basket size, so a reading of 0.47 is neither high nor low on its own. That is why the library never ships a number without a null beside it, and why this article treats the point as a result rather than a caveat.
What the gauge reads on a real market
Here is the tool doing its job before the machinery is explained: the opening of ORC_Scan_Market.mq5 on a twenty-six symbol basket of majors, crosses, metals, energy, indices and crypto, daily bars, a rolling correlation window of 120 bars, stepping five bars at a time from January 2020 to August 2026.
