Three things in those lines are the whole article. The structureless pole of 0.520000 is what a market with no structure whatsoever would read at this basket size; it is a closed form in the node count and nothing else. Real readings sit between 0.4596 and 0.4833, so the readable quantity is the gap below the pole, averaging 0.0449, not the level itself. And the last line speaks to the sharpest objection this library faces: over 385 windows the curvature and the average correlation share 55.6% of their variance, so 44.4% is what a linear fit on the average correlation does not reproduce: strongly related, but not the same column.
The pipeline behind that number has six stages, each one a design decision that could have been made wrong.
Fig. 1. The six stages, and the fact that a reading is a distance between the last two boxes rather than the value produced by the first
The rest explains how, and what happens when each stage is built the obvious way.
Curvature on a graph, in one formula
Start with the geometry and leave the market out of it. Take a graph, pick an edge between nodes x and y, and give each endpoint a probability measure describing its neighborhood: a share alpha of the mass stays on the node, and the remaining 1 - alpha is spread over its neighbors. Now ask how expensive it is to turn m_x into m_y, where moving one unit of mass from u to v costs d(u, v). That cost is the Wasserstein-1 distance, and Ollivier's curvature compares it to the distance between the nodes.
If the neighborhoods overlap heavily, most of the mass is already where it needs to be, so W1 is much smaller than d and kappa is positive. If the edge is the only route between two otherwise separate groups, every unit has to cross it and then travel further, so W1 exceeds d and kappa goes negative. The measure is what makes this work, and the library builds it from the definition.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| One probability measure per node, over its own neighborhood      |
//|                                                                  |
//|     m_x(x) = alpha                                               |
//|     m_x(y) = (1 - alpha) w(x,y) / sum_z w(x,z)                   |
//|                                                                  |
//|  A node with no neighbors keeps all of its mass, which makes the |
//|  transport against it well defined instead of a divide by zero.  |
