The isolated node case is not defensive clutter. A node with no neighbors keeps all of its mass, which makes the transport against it well defined instead of a division by zero, and the sparse graph modes can produce exactly that situation.
Four small graphs pin the sign convention down, and all four have answers that can be worked out by hand, which makes them a test.
Fig. 2. Edge curvature on four shapes at alpha 0.5, drawn from the same routine the tests use: red is negative, grey is zero, blue is positive
On the complete graph every edge reads exactly +0.625, because every pair of neighborhoods is identical. Along a path the interior edges read 0.000 and the end edges +0.500. Every spoke of the seven-node star reads +0.166667. The dumbbell is the finance argument in miniature: two cliques joined by one bridge, where the bridge reads -0.500000 and the clique edges average +0.541667. That bridge is the only way across, so its removal splits the market, and the curvature says so with a sign change rather than a threshold.
Translating this to markets needs a distance, and the standard one comes from Mantegna's 1999 work on correlation-based trees: d(i, j) = sqrt(2 (1 - rho)). Perfectly correlated instruments sit at distance 0, uncorrelated ones at sqrt(2), and perfectly anticorrelated ones at 2. This transform is not a convenience: it is a genuine metric, and two parts of the library are correct only because it satisfies the triangle inequality. The class exposes WorstTriangleSlack() and the tests assert on it, so swapping in another distance formula has to clear that assertion or two speedups silently stop being exact.
The transport problem, solved exactly
W1 between two discrete measures is a transportation problem, a minimum cost flow, and the textbook method is successive shortest augmenting paths with node potentials. There is no iteration count to tune, no convergence tolerance to pick and no regularization weight: the answer that comes back is the answer, and the solver can prove it. There are small fixed epsilons, ORC_EPS, ORC_CEPS and ORC_GEPS at 1.0e-12 and a 1.0e-10 floor on flow read back, but they decide when a residual counts as spent rather than how accurate the answer is, and the certificate does not depend on them. The entropic approximation trades exactness for speed on problems with tens of thousands of points; these have 26.
One property is where the speed comes from. Wherever the two measures already agree, that mass can stay where it is, so only the difference has to be transported:
W1(a, b) = W1( (a-b)+ , (b-a)+ )
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Wasserstein-1 between two measures over the same node set        |
//|                                                                  |
//|  mx and my are full length probability vectors over n nodes and  |
//|  cost is the n x n metric between those nodes. Only the parts    |
//|  where the two disagree are handed to the solver; see the class  |
