Measured on the library's own test data, that reduction takes a typical instance from 99 linear program variables to 24, a shrink of 4.17 times, and cuts the mass that has to move from 1.0 to a median of 0.47 at the shipped alpha of 0.5. It is exact only when the cost is a metric, which is why the Mantegna transform earns its place. The test suite measures that dependency: on the Mantegna distance the full and reduced answers agree to 0.000e+00, and on a cost matrix built to violate the triangle inequality the same shortcut is wrong by up to 2.039e-01.
The solver loop is short, and the line that matters most in it is the iteration guard.
