Read the augmentation columns relative to the problem size. The reduced transport problem for an n-symbol network has about n supplies and demands between them, so the mean sits at roughly 1.5 times (m + n) and the maximum at 3.2 times at the largest size, and the ratio is still creeping upward as the network grows. The classical bound is passed on essentially every solve, and no fixed multiple of it is safe to assume.
That is what sets the guard. A limit of 2(m + n) sits below the observed maximum, and reaching a limit does not complete the flow, it only stops the loop counting: recompiled with that limit, the same sweep leaves 5 edges at n = 24, 70 at n = 30 and 424 at n = 36 failing the certificate, the worst by 4.891e-01. A residual that size is the whole signal this library measures, so such a solve is arithmetic wearing the format of a result. The shipped guard is 16(m + n) + 64, set from the sweep with room to spare: an empirical backstop against cycles, not a proved bound. If it is hit, the solver refuses and returns -1.0, because an inexact kappa is indistinguishable from a market reading.
None of that would be measurable without the unusual part: the solver certifies itself.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| An optimality certificate the solver produces about itself       |
//|                                                                  |
//|  Returns the largest violation of the three conditions that,     |
//|  together, mean the answer is optimal and not merely feasible:   |
//|                                                                  |
//|    1. the plan is feasible - every row sums to its supply and    |
//|       every column to its demand;                                |
//|    2. dual feasibility - pot[j] - pot[i] <= c(i,j) everywhere;   |
//|    3. complementary slackness - wherever mass actually moved,    |
//|       that inequality is tight.                                  |
//|                                                                  |
//|  The certificate needs no second implementation to be right, so a|
//|  test can demand a residual near zero on data no reference solver|
//|  covers. It does not replace an external check: it runs on the   |
//|  same code path and the same data, so it can only catch a plan   |
