Three conditions together mean the answer is optimal, not merely feasible: the plan moves exactly the supplies and fills exactly the demands, the dual inequality holds everywhere, and it is tight wherever mass moved. The value is that it needs no reference implementation: there is no external oracle for 62 400 random transport instances, so a test can demand a near zero residual on data no reference covers. At the shipped guard the worst residual across that sweep is 3.109e-15, with zero refusals and zero bad certificates. It does not replace an external check. Running on the same code path and arrays as the solve, it catches a plan this solver got wrong but never a misreading both share, which is why the HiGHS crosscheck exists and why the sweep is evidence about the instances tested, not a proof for every input.
Twenty-six symbols on one clock
Most indicators read one symbol. This one reads twenty-six, and that is a different problem. A correlation is only meaningful between returns observed over the same interval, so the usable sample is the intersection of the symbols' bar times, not the union and not the longest member. Two consequences were measured on a live broker.
First, the basket's start date is set by its worst member. On daily bars, FX and metals reached back nine years, crypto seven, indices six, and one energy symbol only five, so adding that symbol would have cost every other one four years of history. Second, a symbol on a slightly different calendar deletes rows for the whole basket: across 47 candidates only 1347 of 1702 bar times were common, so 21% of the sample was gone, almost all of it from four symbols, the worst at 86.0% coverage.
Coverage is therefore computed per symbol, offenders are dropped before the intersection, and the reason is kept. The subtlety is what coverage is measured against.
