Measuring coverage over the anchor's whole range would conflate two different faults. A symbol on a different calendar deletes rows from everyone and has to go. A symbol that simply starts later only costs the basket a start date, which the intersection already handles. Asking a broker for a fixed 1200 daily bars gives a seven day a week instrument about 3.3 years and a five day a week one about 4.2, so measured that way crypto scores 85.5% and leaves for being younger rather than misaligned. Restricting the comparison to the calendar span the two histories share keeps it.
The 97% floor that ships is a daily setting; below D1 it deletes the entire point of the basket. On thirty minute bars every metal, every index and oil score 94% to 95% against FX, because their sessions differ, so a 0.97 floor drops 11 of 26 symbols and leaves an FX only network wearing a multi-asset label. On M30 over 2000 bars: 0.97 gives 15 symbols and 1423 rows, while 0.90 gives all 26 for 1322 rows, so the whole multi-asset structure costs 7% of the rows. Use 0.90 intraday, and the class appends a warning to its note whenever a fifth or more of the basket is lost, because a panel row saying "15 of 26" is easy to read straight past.
Important:
a partial basket is not a smaller basket, it is a different network. Fewer nodes move the structureless pole, change every measure and change the answer, so accepting whichever symbols happen to have arrived would report a curvature for twenty-six names while reading sixteen. The class separates "still downloading" from "genuinely dropped" through PendingCount(), and the indicator refuses to compute until that count is zero.
There is a platform constraint underneath that, worth naming for anyone writing multi-symbol code. Sleep does not work in a custom indicator, so a retry loop around CopyRates with a sleep between attempts spins instantly, finds nothing, and reports that the broker offers no history when the series is merely still arriving. The class reads MQL_PROGRAM_TYPE and asks once in an indicator, marking the miss as pending so the caller can retry later.
The two decisions that decide whether the number means anything
Two choices sit between the definition and a usable gauge, and in each case the obvious option carries no information. Neither is settled by taste: each has a closed form or a measurement.
The graph, and a closed form that kills the obvious design
. The natural first move is to make every pair an edge and give every neighbor an equal share of the mass. On a complete graph with a uniform measure, m_x and m_y agree on every node except x and y, so exactly |alpha - (1-alpha)/(n-1)| of mass has to move, and it moves exactly d(x, y). The distance cancels:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| What a market with no structure at all would read                |
//|                                                                  |
//|  A complete graph with a flat measure gives exactly this value,  |
//|  and it depends only on the node count and on alpha. It is the   |
//|  POLE of the scale: real readings sit just below it, and the gap |
//|  rather than the level is what carries information. A line drawn |
//|  at 0.47 says nothing; the same reading as a gap of 0.046 does.  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CORCGraph::StructurelessReference(
const
int
n,
const
double
alpha)
  {
if
(n <
2
)
return
(
1.0
);
return
(
1.0
-
MathAbs
(alpha - (
1.0
- alpha) / (
double
)(n -
1
)));
  }
That configuration cannot see the market at all. It is not a tendency but an identity: confirmed at 5, 8 and 12 nodes and at alpha 0, 0.25, 0.5 and 0.75, the worst error against the closed form is 9.992e-16 and the spread across edges is 2.220e-16. What the gauge needs is therefore not sparsity but a measure that is not uniform: a near neighbor must carry more mass than a far one, or the distances cancel and the reading is a constant.
The obvious repair, making the graph sparse, is worse. Two genuine sectors make a k-nearest-neighbor graph or a threshold graph fall into separate components, so shortest paths become infinite and W1 is undefined on exactly the market this method exists to detect. The k-nearest variant has a second flaw: it only reacts once the neighbor ordering changes, which makes it a step detector, flat from an inter-sector correlation of 0.0 to 0.6 and then jumping.
The shipped design gives the two roles to two different objects. The cost stays the full Mantegna metric, so it is always finite, and no shortest path pass is needed because on a complete graph over a metric the direct arc is already cheapest. The measure is what becomes non uniform, giving a closer neighbor more mass through a Gaussian decay.
