Measured under a doubling of every distance in the matrix, a fixed bandwidth of 0.7 moves kappa by 0.3061, while the window's own median moves it by 4.4e-16. That is not a smoothing preference, it is the difference between a scale-free statistic and one that tracks the correlation level.
Three curvature aggregates appear below and only one is the reading. Kappa is the unweighted mean over every scored edge, all 325 on a twenty-six symbol basket, and it is what the gap is measured from and what the nulls are compared against. Node curvature is the mean over a symbol's own edges, the only form an expert can act on instrument by instrument. The struct also carries kappaWeighted, each edge weighted by its absolute correlation, and nothing consumes it: it is a diagnostic, since weighting by correlation would reintroduce the level dependence the two decisions remove.
With both decisions made correctly the central claim can be tested, narrowly. Curvature is a ratio of a distance to a distance and W1 is linear in the cost matrix, so multiplying every distance by one constant leaves kappa bit-identical. That is a proof about rescaling, and rescaling is not what a rising market does: Mantegna's map is concave, so adding 0.05 to every correlation shortens an edge at rho 0.9 by 29% and one at rho 0.1 by 2.8%. The network is bent, tight part compressed hardest, not scaled. What survives is a measurement, not a corollary.
Fig. 4. Two sectors merging move the gauge by 0.0415 across six points, while a single driver dialed from rho 0.1 to 0.9 moves it by 0.0013, which is sampling noise between independent draws rather than a response
The library's own test runs a tighter version of the same pair, three points each with matched seeds, measuring the shape sweep at 0.0415 against a level sweep 261 times smaller, printed as 0.0002. At a fixed shape, dialing the level moves the gauge by about what resampling noise moves it, so "everything became more correlated" is close to invisible to this statistic. That is a measured near-invariance on synthetic markets, not a structural one, and the real basket below puts a number on what survives.
The nulls, including the one that is empty
Choosing the null is where most of the thinking went, and the first candidate is instructive because it is completely empty. In the anomaly detection and dependence libraries I have built for this site, the sharpest control is to shuffle the bars in time, destroying only the ordering. That control cannot work here. Permuting the rows of a return matrix leaves every column pair correlation algebraically unchanged, so it produces the identical correlation matrix, distance matrix and curvature: reporting it as a passed control would be reporting that a number equals itself. The data test asserts exactly this, so a control that cannot move the number is never counted as a success: a joint shuffle moves kappa by 0.0e+00, while shuffling each symbol separately moves it by 2.5e-02.
Two nulls do carry information. The independent null permutes each symbol's returns on its own, so every symbol keeps its own distribution, fat tails included, and only the cross-sectional structure is destroyed. At a finite window this does not read as the structureless pole, because sampling noise alone builds correlation, which makes it the honest "no relationships, this many observations" reference. The one-factor null matters more.
