Five points across six and a half years, twenty draws each. Real curvature sits 49 to 83 standard deviations below the one-factor band, and 5 of 5 windows fall below the fifth percentile of both nulls. Read the sd figures as an order of magnitude: twenty draws fix the band's mean well and its spread only roughly, so what they support is "far outside the null" rather than "-67.9" as a measured quantity, while the 5 of 5 count needs no spread estimate. The null is a reference, not a model. Its draw is Gaussian on one positive factor, so it reproduces neither the real tails nor the negative pairs a real basket carries, and another matrix with the same average absolute correlation could have a different spectrum. What survives is the direction: a real matrix is not a blob.
One more thing travels with every reading. A high curvature is ambiguous, because a market with almost no relationships and one collapsed into a single undifferentiated blob both lack cluster structure. The level sweep measures it on a fourteen node synthetic basket: at rho 0.1 it reads 0.533545 and at rho 0.9 it reads 0.533490. The scale depends on the basket size: fourteen nodes put the pole at 0.538462 rather than the 0.520000 of the twenty-six symbol basket, so both sit about 0.005 below their own pole, not above it. Only the average correlation separates the two cases, which is why the band is a floor to stay below rather than a target, and why every reading carries meanAbsCorr.
