Load a basket, ask for a reading, ask for a null, and read the gap. Symbols() and DroppedReport() are there so a caller finds out that the basket is not what was asked for rather than wondering why the scan is short.
ORC_Fragility.mq5 plots the curvature in a subwindow with the one-factor band as two dotted levels, and a panel carrying the average correlation, the gap from the pole, the standardized distance from the band and a verdict. Three properties of that display were each a decision.
The plot is a step. One window over twenty-six symbols is 325 exact transport problems, so the reading is computed every InpStep bars and held in between. A held bar carries the previous reading, not an interpolation, and the reading changes on average every 5.3 bars against a declared step of five.
The band is computed once, at the newest bar, and drawn as levels. A null draw costs a full curvature computation, so fifteen draws cost fifteen windows: affordable once, not per bar.
Every plotted point comes from its own causal window and is never revisited. The indicator walks chart bars and assigns the newest reading at or before each one, because the basket is an intersection with fewer rows than the chart has bars. Stepping over basket rows instead leaves a hole at every bar the basket does not contain.
Important:
